# WCAG Guideline 4: Robust

**Robust** is the fourth and final WCAG principle. It means your content must work reliably across different browsers, devices, and assistive technologies, both now and as those tools evolve in the future. The formal wording is that content must be robust enough to be interpreted reliably by a wide variety of user agents, including assistive technologies.

In practice, the way we achieve robustness is by writing valid, semantically appropriate HTML, making sure status messages are exposed to assistive technology, and reaching for ARIA only when native HTML cannot express what we need.

## A Concrete Failure: divs and spans That Skip the Keyboard

Here is a real example of violating this guideline. Imagine a conference program guide that lists sessions you can click into, add to a schedule, or add to a reading list. It looks correct on screen because a lot of CSS was applied to get the right appearance. The trouble is that the whole interface was built out of `<div>` and `<span>` elements chosen for how they *look*, not for what they *mean*.

```html
<div class="program">
  <div class="session" onclick="openSession()">
    <span class="title">Designing for Accessibility</span>
    <span class="speaker">A. Researcher</span>
  </div>
  <!-- ...many more div "sessions"... -->
</div>
```

When a keyboard user selects the top of the page and presses <kbd>Tab</kbd> to move down to a specific day, the focus jumps right past the entire program. A `<div>` is not focusable and carries no role, so the browser and screen reader have nothing to stop on and nothing to announce. The visual design worked, but the interface was unusable without a mouse. Rebuilding it from semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<button>`, `<a>`, `<ul>`/`<li>`) fixes this, because those elements come with focus behavior and roles built in.

## Success Criterion 4.1.2: Name, Role, Value

The core requirement lives in **SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)**. For every user interface component, including form elements, links, and controls generated by scripts:

- Its **name** and **role** can be *programmatically determined* (assistive tech can read what it is and what it is called).
- Its **states, properties, and values** that a user can set can be *programmatically set* (for example, whether a checkbox is checked).
- **Notification of changes** to those items is available to user agents, including assistive technologies. This is why a status message like "3 items added to cart" or a form error must be exposed so a screen reader announces it, not only shown visually.

A real `<button>` satisfies all of this automatically: its role is `button`, its name comes from its text, and it is keyboard-focusable and clickable. A `<div onclick=...>` satisfies none of it.

## Semantic Structure

```{html}
<nav aria-label="Main navigation">
  <a href="/lessons">Lessons</a>
  <a href="/assignments">Assignments</a>
</nav>
```

- The `nav` element gives the region a landmark role, so assistive tech can jump straight to navigation.
- The `aria-label` gives that landmark a useful, programmatically determinable name, which matters when a page has more than one `nav`.

## Do Not Fight the Platform

If a native HTML element already has the behavior and meaning you need, use it. A real `<button>` is more robust than a custom clickable element that imitates a button, because the native control brings focusability, keyboard activation, and a role that assistive technology understands.

Native HTML cannot express *everything*, though. When you build a widget that has no native equivalent (a tab panel, a live-updating status region, a custom combobox), you fill the gap with the **ARIA standard**, which adds the missing semantic information for assistive technologies. That is the subject of the next lesson.

## Exercises

1. What does "robust" mean in WCAG?

   ```{mcq}
   What is this principle about?
   correct: Correct. Robust content works reliably with current and future user agents and assistive technologies.
   wrong: Think about compatibility with browsers and assistive tools, now and in the future.
   [x] Content works reliably with current and future user agents and assistive technologies.
   [ ] Content only works in one browser version.
   [ ] Content avoids semantic HTML.
   [ ] Content hides all controls.
   ```

2. The card below is a clickable `<div>`, so a keyboard user cannot focus or activate it. Replace it with a real `<button>` element (keep the text "Add to schedule") so its role and name are programmatically determinable.

   ```{html}
   <div class="action" onclick="addToSchedule()">Add to schedule</div>
   ```
   ```+solution
   <button onclick="addToSchedule()">Add to schedule</button>
   ```
   ```+test
   assert('button').should('exist').should('have.text', 'Add to schedule').run('Replace the div with a real <button> whose text is "Add to schedule"', 'The control is now a native button with a determinable role and name');
   ```

3. Add an accessible name to the navigation landmark using `aria-label="Main navigation"`.

   ```{html}
   <nav>
     <a href="/lessons">Lessons</a>
   </nav>
   ```
   ```+solution
   <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
     <a href="/lessons">Lessons</a>
   </nav>
   ```
   ```+test
   wrap(source.includes('aria-label="Main navigation"') || source.includes("aria-label='Main navigation'")).should('equal', true).run('Add `aria-label="Main navigation"` to the nav element', 'Named the navigation landmark');
   ```

4. According to SC 4.1.2, what must be true of every user interface component?

   ```{mcq}
   What has to be discoverable about a control?
   correct: Correct. Its name and role must be programmatically determinable, user-settable values must be programmatically settable, and changes must be exposed to assistive technologies.
   wrong: Think about what a screen reader needs to know about a control: what it is, what it is called, its current value, and when that value changes.
   [x] Its name and role can be programmatically determined, and changes to its state are exposed to assistive technologies.
   [ ] It must be built from div and span elements styled with CSS.
   [ ] It must be hidden from assistive technologies.
   [ ] It must avoid using native HTML elements.
   ```

